{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Then his master shall bring him to the Elohim</b> According to Rashi, it is necessary that he consult those who sold him, who sold him to him (Mekhilta). And Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra said (on Exodus 21:6) that the judges are called Elohim because they uphold the judgments of Elohim on earth. And in my opinion, Scripture says <b>then his master shall bring him to the Elohim</b>, \"until the matter comes to the Elohim\" (Exodus 22:8), to hint that Elohim will be with them in the matter of judgment — He acquits and He condemns — and this is what it says (there) \"whom the Elohim condemn,\" and thus Moses said, \"for the judgment is Elohim's\" (Deuteronomy 1:17). And thus Jehoshaphat said, \"for you judge not for man but for HaShem, and He is with you in the matter of judgment\" (II Chronicles 19:6). And so Scripture said, \"Elohim stands in the congregation of El, in the midst of Elohim He judges\" (Psalms 82:1), meaning: in the midst of the congregation of Elohim He judges, for Elohim is the judge. And likewise it says, \"then both men who have the dispute shall stand before HaShem\" (Deuteronomy 19:17). And this is the meaning of \"for I will not acquit the wicked\" (Exodus 23:7) according to the correct interpretation. And in Shemot Rabbah (30:24) I found: only at the time when a judge sits and judges in truth, the Holy One, blessed be He, as it were, sets aside the name of Heaven and causes His Shekhinah to dwell at his side, as it is said (Judges 2:18), \"for HaShem raised up judges for them, and HaShem was with the judge.\"" "<b>and he shall serve him forever.</b> Our Sages explained (in the Mekhilta here) that this means until the Jubilee. And Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra said (on Exodus 21:6) that the meaning of \"forever\" (עולם) is a period of time in the language of Scripture, as in \"it has already been for ages (לעולמים) that which was before us\" (Ecclesiastes 1:10) — periods of time; \"and he sat there forever (עד עולם)\" (I Samuel 1:22). Therefore it says <b>and he shall serve him forever</b>, for the period of the Jubilee, since among the appointed times of Israel there is no span longer than it, and the going out to freedom is as though eternity is renewed for him, and its meaning will be that he returns to his original state when he was free. And the enlightened will understand that \"forever\" is according to its literal meaning, for the one who serves until the Jubilee has served all the days of the world. And the language of the Mekhilta (here) is: Rabbi says, come and see that there is no עולם except fifty years, as it is said, <b>and he shall serve him forever</b>, until the Jubilee. And Rabbi Abraham forgot what he had insightfully written in another place."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {1 "The closing line 'ושכח ר\"א מה שהשכיל וכתב במקום אחר' is rendered literally; the reference is to Ibn Ezra's own comment elsewhere where he acknowledged that עולם can mean eternity, contradicting his claim here."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:18:03.234038237Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}